## Why This Is Asked
Tests real-world concurrency and multi-region resilience for a streaming AI workload beyond typical batch jobs.

## Key Concepts
- Tail latency, autoscaling, per-tenant QoS, cache effectiveness, end-to-end tracing
- Backpressure handling, failure injection, graceful degradation
- Serverless vs. reserved compute trade-offs in a streaming path

## Code Example
```javascript
import ws from 'k6/ws';
import { check } from 'k6';
export default function () {
  const url = 'wss://transcription.example.com/stream';
  const s = ws.connect(url, {}, (socket) => {
    socket.on('open', () => socket.send(JSON.stringify({type:'start'})));
    socket.on('message', (msg) => {});
  });
  check(s, { 'connected': (r) => r && r.status === 101 });
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you model per-tenant QoS and quotas in your workload generators?
- What metrics and thresholds would you use to trigger autoscaling up and down?

## Why This Is Asked

This probes ability to design practical experiments that separate database work from front-end rendering, a common real-world pain point.

## Key Concepts

- Tail latency and SLA adherence
- End-to-end vs component latency
- Cache warmth effects and warm/cold runs
- Baseline and delta analysis

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-measurement sketch
async function measure(endpoint){
  const t0=performance.now();
  await fetch(endpoint);
  const t1=performance.now();
  return t1-t0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend this to multi-tenant workloads?
- How would you validate that caching changes don’t degrade data freshness?
